      Mercury:  g_N = 3.9575e-02 m/s^2,  g_N/a0 = 3.298e+08,  f_threshold = sqrt(a0/g_N) = 5.507e-05,  min |dg/g_N| at f=f_Cassini = 4.357e-05
       Saturn:  g_N = 6.5197e-05 m/s^2,  g_N/a0 = 5.433e+05,  f_threshold = sqrt(a0/g_N) = 1.357e-03,  min |dg/g_N| at f=f_Cassini = 1.345e-03
  [PASS] P4-1  *** KERNEL-INDEPENDENT: at the Cassini-allowed f = 1.15e-5 the scalar is deep-MOND at Saturn for EVERY a0 footing (f << sqrt(a0/g_N) ~ 1.2e-3), and therefore the fractional anomalous acceleration at Saturn is AT LEAST ~1.2e-3 -- about a tenth of a percent of Newtonian gravity -- for ANY interpolating kernel obeying mu(y) <= y. ***
           (repo canonical 9.36e-11: threshold 1.198e-03, min anomaly 1.187e-03; repo alt      1.13e-10: threshold 1.317e-03, min anomaly 1.305e-03; c^2/(2 pi L_dS) 1.2e-10: threshold 1.357e-03, min anomaly 1.345e-03)
  [PASS] P4-2a  the cancellation-free anomaly dg = f g_N / expm1(y) agrees with the direct subtraction g_s - f g_N to 1e-6 in the regime where the subtraction is still numerically safe (small y); beyond that only the stable form is usable, which is why the earlier naive column read 0
           (r=9.54AU f=1.2e-05: direct 8.788981e-08 vs stable 8.788981e-08; r=9.54AU f=1.0e-03: direct 4.395388e-08 vs stable 4.395388e-08; r=0.39AU f=1.5e-04: direct 3.542204e-09 vs stable 3.542204e-09)
  [PASS] P4-2  the theorem's bound is verified numerically for all four kernels at Saturn, f = 1.15e-5: every kernel produces an anomaly >= sqrt(a0/g_N) - f, and the exponential kernel SATURATES it (so the exponential kernel -- the branch's own choice -- is the most favourable possible)
